3. what the 2-adic structure gives UNCONDITIONALLY
============================================================================
  The 2^t residues are r_w = base + sum_{i in B(w)} s_i mod 64^t
  with v_2(s_i) = 3 + 6i exactly.  So if w, w' first differ at
  index k then 2^{3+6k} divides r_w - r_w'.
  verified v_2(s_i) = 3+6i for t = 4, 8, 12 and every i.

  THEOREM (new here).  Two starts below H_t = 2^{5t+1} cannot agree
  on their first K letters, where K = ceil((5t-2)/6): they would
  differ by a nonzero multiple of 2^{3+6K} >= 2^{5t+1}, which
  exceeds the width of the interval.  Hence
        B_t <= 2^K,   K = ceil((5t-2)/6) ~ 5t/6.
  Refining by prefix length k gives B_t <= 2^{5t-2-5k} + 2^k, whose
  optimum is again k = (5t-2)/6.  So 5t/6 is the CEILING of the
  purely 2-adic argument, not an artefact of the first proof.

  t    B_t      K        2^K        min first-diff index
  4    2        3        8          0
  6    2        5        32         2
  8    4        7        128        1
  10   2        8        256        0
  12   6        10       1024       0
  14   1        12       4096       -
  16   2        13       8192       2
  The observed first-difference indices are far below K, so the
  bound 2^K is nowhere near attained -- but nothing in the 2-adic
  structure sees that.  Closing the gap from 2^{5t/6} to 14t is
  exactly an equidistribution statement for {r_w}.

